# Adjudicated `punused` findings for deadset-go. One reason per group; entries
# match as literal substrings of the finding line, so they never include
# file:line:col -- line numbers shift and an entry with one silently goes stale.
#
# `punused` resolves references through gopls inside this module only and does
# not model interface satisfaction, so two whole classes below are its known
# blind spots rather than dead code. Cross-checked at adjudication time:
# `deadcode -test ./...` reports nothing in this module.
#
# Invoke it as `punused` or `punused '**/*.go'`. A package pattern reports
# nothing and exits 0, which reads exactly like a clean scan.

# --- Fixture modules under internal/load/testdata/.
#
# Each of these is a declaration inside a fixture module the loader's own tests
# load and analyze. They are deliberately unreferenced: the fixtures exist to be
# read by a package loader, not called, and two of them are deliberately
# ill-formed (a type mismatch and an undefined identifier) so the fail-closed
# error walk has something to walk. The three in the consumed target are the
# declarations a loaded consumer references, so the module that declares them
# names none of them itself, which is the shape the consumer load is about. Go's
# own tooling ignores a `testdata` directory; this scan does not, so the fixtures
# surface here as findings. Deleting any of them would break the test that names
# it.
function Selected
function Answer
function Mismatch
function Undefined
function Used is unused
function UsedByConsumerTest
function Unused is unused

# --- Methods a standard interface calls, never a caller by name.
#
# `Error()` on both error types satisfies `error`, and production code returns
# them as `error`; `String()` on the kind enumerations satisfies `fmt.Stringer`
# and is what renders a kind in a message and in a report; `Unwrap()` is what
# `errors.Is` and `errors.As` call to walk a chain; `MarshalJSON` and
# `UnmarshalJSON` on the report envelope satisfy `json.Marshaler` and
# `json.Unmarshaler`, which is what makes marshalling an envelope write the
# Contract's document and reading one refuse a member the schema does not
# declare. No call site exists for a reference search to find in any of these
# cases, because the runtime dispatches through the interface. Removing one would
# not fail the build, it would silently change what a diagnostic prints, which
# errors match, or which bytes a report is written as, which is why each is
# pinned by a test of its rendered output, of the match, or of the round trip.
method (*Error).Error
method (ErrorKind).String
method (RefKind).String
method (*MalformedError).Unwrap
method (*Envelope).MarshalJSON
method (*Envelope).UnmarshalJSON

# --- What a reporting verb calls, exercised by tests until one exists.
#
# The analysis is assembled: the composition root reads the suppression documents
# and the declared edges, runs the matrix, the exemption classes, the sweep, the
# emitters table and the self-check rules, and assembles the report envelope the
# Contract declares. What has no production caller is the step AFTER that
# envelope. No verb prints a report yet, so the five reporters and the two
# orderings the envelope is printed through are reached by their tests alone; no
# flag writes a baseline; and no verb returns a verdict, so nothing counts the
# pending findings an exit code is decided by. Each is named in the plan for the
# verb that lands it, and the correct resolution is that call site rather than an
# unexport: a reporter is the report package's surface, and the packages are
# separate so that one stage's failure cannot break another's compile.
function Annotations is used in test only
function JSON is used in test only
function SARIF is used in test only
function Template is used in test only
function Text is used in test only
function Sort is used in test only
function Cap is used in test only
function WriteBaseline is used in test only
function Pending is used in test only

# --- The three kinds whose subject is not a declaration of the inventory.
#
# Each has a rule, and none of the three can be registered in the emitters table:
# the findings framework identifies a finding by the declaration of the inventory
# its subject reference names, and a file no configuration built, a module
# requirement and a module directive are none of them. Registering one fails
# every run of the analyzer rather than reporting nothing, so the table names them
# as kinds without an entry and its own test says which they are. Each is reached
# by its tests until the framework admits a subject that is not a declaration,
# and the correct resolution is that admission rather than an unexport.
function FileNeverBuilt is used in test only
function UnusedDependency is used in test only
function UnusedReplace is used in test only

# --- The per-analyzer configuration section.
#
# `Go` is the section of the configuration document this analyzer owns, declared
# in the published schema and therefore accepted by the decode. Nothing reads it
# yet because no setting inside it has an implementation; the field must exist
# for a document that carries the section to resolve rather than be refused.
field Go is unused

# --- What the sweep answers and no verb reports yet.
#
# `print-retained` indexes the graph from the enumeration, the reference set and
# the root set and sweeps it, so the index and the sweep have callers. What no
# verb reads yet is a dead component: the listing at the depth the run asks for,
# the spelling a message carries for a cascade, and which consumers reference one
# symbol, which the graph records per symbol against the day a finding names them
# one by one. Only the tests call them today, and the correct resolution is the
# reporting verb rather than an unexport, because each is a stage of the same
# package the enumeration and the roots are stages of.
method (*Component).List
method (Cascade).String
method (*Graph).ConsumersOf
